=== main.frg
interface Attacker {
    int power();
}

interface Mover {
    void move();
}

abstract class Enemy {
    abstract int reward();
}

class Goblin : Enemy, Attacker {
    string power() {
        return "big";
    }
}

class Rock {
}

class Orc : Enemy {
    int reward() {
        return super.reward() + 1;
    }
}

class Bat : Rock, Enemy {
}

class Slime : Mover, Mover {
    void move() {
    }
}

int main(){
    Attacker a = Attacker();
    Enemy e = Enemy();
    return 0;
}
=== error
'reward' is abstract in Enemy, so it has no code to call
a class can only inherit from one class, but Bat already inherits from Rock
'Mover' is listed twice
'power' has to match the one in Attacker
Goblin has to have the method 'reward', because Enemy (an abstract class it inherits from) says so
Attacker is an interface, so it can't be made directly
Enemy is an abstract class, so it can't be made directly
